              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to stay in the country, then the Latin for lovely."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: cantus, cantūs",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cantus, cantūs — \"singing, song; music\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Cantum avium māne audīmus. In the morning we hear the song of the birds."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Masculine, fourth declension, from canō. Singing, and playing too. English chant comes from the verb cantō."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cantus is the action noun of canō \"to sing\", inherited from a Proto-Indo-European root meaning \"to sing\" (EtymDB). English chant belongs to the same family."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
